<h2>Proposal Overview — Why Now, What</h2>
<p>Developers keep doing exactly what they do now — landing their work in GitLab — while <strong>AI analyzes those results and automatically converts and accumulates them as organizational knowledge</strong>. This proposes a <strong>Read-Only Engineering Knowledge Platform</strong>. It is not a wiki that people write; it is a structure in which <strong>the development artifacts themselves are the source of knowledge</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">In one sentence</span>
    A <strong>Read-Only Engineering Knowledge Platform</strong> in which AI continuously analyzes the development artifacts accumulating in GitLab and converts them into the organization's <strong>currently valid development knowledge</strong>.
</div>

<p>And this is not a blank-sheet design. The knowledge storage, indexing, and linking engine of this proposal is <strong>already implemented and proven in the Reference Wiki</strong> that runs as an advance validation — as of 2026-07, measured: 115 documents · 370 unique concepts · 364 automatic related edges · 114 human-declared typed knowledge relations, all running on top of a <a href="#!kgs-overview">deterministic registration pipeline</a>. The <a href="#!dzp-knowledge">Knowledge Management</a> and <a href="#!dzp-security">Asset Utilization</a> installments of this series cover how that engine scales to company size. In other words, this proposal is not imagining "what could be built" but describing <strong>"how to connect a proven engine already in operation to GitLab"</strong>.</p>

<h3>The Final Picture — A Virtuous Cycle</h3>
<p>The goal is not the wiki itself. It is a <strong>virtuous cycle</strong> in which development results become knowledge, and the accumulated knowledge in turn raises the productivity of the next development. A wiki written by people demanded the extra labor of "develop → write a document → register it in the Wiki" and never lasted; in this structure development experience becomes an organizational asset, repeated analysis shrinks, experience is reused across projects, onboarding gets faster, design intent is preserved, and AI tools such as Claude Code use organizational knowledge as Context.</p>

